Insert a keyboard's long or multi-line commit as one paste.

An input method that pastes from its own clipboard, as the suggestion strip of
Gboard does, commits the text in one piece, and NativeEditContext typed it:
viewController.type runs every character through the typing handlers, a model
edit for each, and a line break goes through Enter with auto indent on top of
the indentation the text already carries. So pasted code came out indented
again at every line, and a few thousand characters held the page for seconds.

On Android a plain commit (no text replaced and no caret moved by the input
method) of more than 64 characters, or of any text with a line break before its
end, is now passed to viewController.paste, which inserts it in one edit and
leaves it as it is. A commit of one character, an Enter, and a composition are
untouched, and so is a word committed together with the Enter that follows it:
that is typing, and Enter has to indent.

Measured on an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4, tapping the clipboard
suggestion above its keys with the text in a file that was already open. 4720
characters of code: the handler of the commit took 1136 ms as typed and 3 ms
as one paste. Five lines of nested braces indented by four spaces came out at
8, 16 and 20 spaces as typed and unchanged as one paste. A DOM paste event,
which Ctrl+V and a long-press Paste use, was never the slow path.

